      "dom_id": "question-23909",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Consider the equation ${x}^{2}+1=k.$ For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-1} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\nk-1<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-1 & <0 \\\\ k & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $k$ that is less than $1$ will give an equation with no real solutions.",
