      "dom_id": "question-222414",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which value of $h$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}-h=2?$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-h & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2+h \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2+h} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{2+h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $h$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+h & =0 \\\\ h & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h=-2$ will give only one solution to the equation.",
